% The kind warrant, pinned. docs/SQL-KINDS.md is the statement of it.
%
%   SQL never reports a match for a row SEL would have refused.
%
% SEL raises E_NOT_NUM for text that is not a number, and the server does not:
% CAST('x' AS DECIMAL) is 0 on MariaDB, MySQL and SQLite, so `T == 0` over a text
% column matched every row and `T == 25` matched every "25/..." value. Reported
% from the field against a MariaDB EAV schema.
%
% An operand nobody has vouched for is therefore wrapped, so a value SEL would
% refuse becomes NULL -- which is not selected, which is what SEL failing has to
% look like from SQL. Declaring the binding NUM buys the plain comparison back,
% and it is the only thing that does: that declaration is where the promise
% transfers from this layer to the application.
%
% What this file does NOT cover is the same defect in a function argument:
% ABS(T), ROUND(T, 2) and MAX(T, 1) all still emit bare over a text column.
% Closing that needs a table of which argument of which function is read as a
% number, and nothing records it today. See docs/SQL-KINDS.md 4.1a.

### name: warrant.numeric.a-text-column-is-guarded
--- note
The reported shape. Was `(CAST(`t` AS DECIMAL(65,10)) = 25)`, which MariaDB
answers TRUE for the stored value "25/298".
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"T": {"kind": "column", "column": "t", "type": "TEXT"}}
--- source
T == 25
--- expect
(CASE WHEN (`t` REGEXP '\\A-?[0-9]+(\\.[0-9]+)?\\z') THEN CAST(`t` AS DECIMAL(65,10)) ELSE NULL END = 25)
===
### name: warrant.numeric.either-side-is-guarded
--- note
Neither side of a numeric operator is privileged, and every other case here puts
the column on the left. Without this one a guard applied to the left operand
only would pass the whole file.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"T": {"kind": "column", "column": "t", "type": "TEXT"}}
--- source
25 == T
--- expect
(25 = CASE WHEN (`t` REGEXP '\\A-?[0-9]+(\\.[0-9]+)?\\z') THEN CAST(`t` AS DECIMAL(65,10)) ELSE NULL END)
===
### name: warrant.numeric.an-undeclared-column-is-guarded
--- note
UNKNOWN is the default binding type, and under this warrant it is the safe
default rather than the silent one: not declaring gets the guard, and declaring
NUM is what opts out of it.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"U": {"kind": "column", "column": "u"}}
--- source
U == 25
--- expect
(CASE WHEN (`u` REGEXP '\\A-?[0-9]+(\\.[0-9]+)?\\z') THEN CAST(`u` AS DECIMAL(65,10)) ELSE NULL END = 25)
===
### name: warrant.numeric.a-declared-num-is-not-guarded
--- note
The other side, and the incentive. A binding that says NUM is vouched for, so
the comparison is plain and the column keeps its index -- the guard is a
function of the column and would lose it.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"N": {"kind": "column", "column": "n", "type": "NUM"}}
--- source
N == 25
--- expect
(`n` = 25)
===
### name: warrant.numeric.a-constant-is-not-guarded
--- note
A constant is settled at translation time by the const.numeric.* family -- it
either IS a number or the expression is refused -- so guarding it would ask the
server a question already answered here, and would cost a bound value a second
parameter for the repeated slot.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"N": {"kind": "column", "column": "n", "type": "NUM"}}
--- source
N == "5"
--- expect
(CAST(`n` AS DECIMAL(65,10)) = CAST('5' AS DECIMAL(65,10)))
===
### name: warrant.numeric.postgresql-asks-through-a-text-cast
--- note
The same question in the dialect's own spelling, and the one entry that fills a
lexical from inside another: `~` needs text, so the operand is cast before it is
tested. The pattern is SEL's numeral grammar, and tools/gen-sql-map.mjs requires
it to match funcs.ISNUM, which sql/oracle/ checks against a real server.
--- dialect
postgresql
--- bindings
{"T": {"kind": "column", "column": "t", "type": "TEXT"}}
--- source
T == 25
--- expect
(CASE WHEN (CAST("t" AS TEXT) ~ '^-?[0-9]+(\.[0-9]+)?$') THEN CAST("t" AS NUMERIC) ELSE NULL END = 25)
===
### name: warrant.numeric.sqlite-cannot-ask-and-refuses
--- note
SQLite has no REGEXP and ANSI has no regex, so neither can ask whether a value is
a SEL number -- which is why funcs.ISNUM is unmapped on both, in the map's own
words. A dialect that cannot ask declares no numericGuard, and the absence is the
refusal: better no SQL than SQL that answers where SEL would not.

This is the one place the warrant takes functionality away rather than making it
safer. Declaring the binding NUM translates it again.
--- dialect
sqlite
--- bindings
{"U": {"kind": "column", "column": "u"}}
--- source
U == 25
--- error
E_SQL_UNSUPPORTED 1:1
===
### name: warrant.numeric.unary-minus-is-guarded
--- note
The unary path, which had no case at all: every other case here reaches the
guard through binary(), so deleting unary()'s call left the whole suite green.
A guard that nothing would miss is not a guard.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"T": {"kind": "column", "column": "t", "type": "TEXT"}}
--- source
-T > 0
--- expect
((-CASE WHEN (`t` REGEXP '\\A-?[0-9]+(\\.[0-9]+)?\\z') THEN CAST(`t` AS DECIMAL(65,10)) ELSE NULL END) > 0)
===
### name: warrant.bool.not-over-an-undeclared-column
--- note
requireBool has three call sites and only the AND/OR/XOR one was exercised.
This is NOT, which reads its operand as a boolean for the same reason and had
nothing watching it.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"U": {"kind": "column", "column": "u"}}
--- source
NOT U
--- error
E_SQL_SHAPE 1:5
===
### name: warrant.bool.an-aggregate-condition-is-not-a-boolean
--- note
And the third site: an aggregate's body is a condition, so an undeclared column
used as one is refused there too. ANY rather than FILTER because both take the
same path and ANY reads more plainly as a condition.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"V": {"kind": "columns", "items": [{"column": "a"}, {"column": "b"}]}, "U": {"kind": "column", "column": "u"}}
--- source
ANY(V, U)
--- error
E_SQL_SHAPE 1:8
===
### name: warrant.bool.an-undeclared-column-is-not-a-boolean
--- note
The other half of the warrant. MariaDB answers `1 AND TRUE` as TRUE, so an
undeclared column holding 1 matched a row SEL refuses with E_NOT_BOOL, and
PostgreSQL raises 42804 instead. A refusal and not a guard, because no dialect
can ask "is this a boolean": in the MySQL family a boolean IS a TINYINT, so
testing IN (0, 1) would also admit a NUM column that SEL refuses.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"U": {"kind": "column", "column": "u"}}
--- source
U AND TRUE
--- error
E_SQL_SHAPE 1:1
===
### name: warrant.numeric.the-guard-reaches-into-a-relation-body
--- note
The shape the report actually came from: an EAV table, one row per value, read
through an aggregate. The guard applies to the field inside the correlated
subquery, so a row whose value is not a number contributes NULL, is not TRUE,
and does not make the EXISTS match.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"ITEMS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "oi", "alias": "oi", "fields": {"QTY": {"table": "oi", "column": "qty", "type": "TEXT"}}, "correlate": "`oi`.`o`=`o`.`id`"}}
--- source
ANY(ITEMS, _["QTY"] == 25)
--- expect
EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM `oi` `oi` WHERE `oi`.`o`=`o`.`id` AND ((CASE WHEN (`oi`.`qty` REGEXP '\\A-?[0-9]+(\\.[0-9]+)?\\z') THEN CAST(`oi`.`qty` AS DECIMAL(65,10)) ELSE NULL END = 25)) IS TRUE)
### name: warrant.numeric.abs-over-a-text-column
--- note
docs/SQL-KINDS.md §4.1a. Every function that reads an argument as a number
guards an argument that is not known to be NUM.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"T": {"kind": "column", "column": "t", "type": "TEXT"}}
--- source
ABS(T)
--- expect
ABS(CASE WHEN (`t` REGEXP '\\A-?[0-9]+(\\.[0-9]+)?\\z') THEN CAST(`t` AS DECIMAL(65,10)) ELSE NULL END)
===
### name: warrant.numeric.sqlite-refuses-abs-over-a-text-column
--- note
On a dialect with no numericGuard, an undeclared/TEXT argument in a numeric
position is refused rather than passed to SQL unguarded.
--- dialect
sqlite
--- bindings
{"T": {"kind": "column", "column": "t", "type": "TEXT"}}
--- source
ABS(T)
--- error
E_SQL_UNSUPPORTED
===
### name: warrant.numeric.round-over-a-text-column
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"T": {"kind": "column", "column": "t", "type": "TEXT"}}
--- source
ROUND(T, 2)
--- expect
ROUND(CASE WHEN (`t` REGEXP '\\A-?[0-9]+(\\.[0-9]+)?\\z') THEN CAST(`t` AS DECIMAL(65,10)) ELSE NULL END, 2)
===
### name: warrant.numeric.max-over-a-text-column
--- note
Two arguments, one of them a plain number, and the other is guarded.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"T": {"kind": "column", "column": "t", "type": "TEXT"}}
--- source
MAX(T, 1)
--- expect
GREATEST(CASE WHEN (`t` REGEXP '\\A-?[0-9]+(\\.[0-9]+)?\\z') THEN CAST(`t` AS DECIMAL(65,10)) ELSE NULL END, 1)
===
### name: warrant.numeric.floor-over-a-text-column
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"T": {"kind": "column", "column": "t", "type": "TEXT"}}
--- source
FLOOR(T)
--- expect
FLOOR(CASE WHEN (`t` REGEXP '\\A-?[0-9]+(\\.[0-9]+)?\\z') THEN CAST(`t` AS DECIMAL(65,10)) ELSE NULL END)
===
### name: warrant.numeric.power-over-a-text-column
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"T": {"kind": "column", "column": "t", "type": "TEXT"}}
--- source
POWER(T, 2)
--- expect
POWER(CASE WHEN (`t` REGEXP '\\A-?[0-9]+(\\.[0-9]+)?\\z') THEN CAST(`t` AS DECIMAL(65,10)) ELSE NULL END, 2)
===
### name: warrant.numeric.a-text-column-as-a-length
--- note
And the argument that is numeric while the others are not: LEFT reads its second
as a count.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"T": {"kind": "column", "column": "t", "type": "TEXT"}}
--- source
LEFT("abc", T)
--- expect
LEFT('abc', CASE WHEN (`t` REGEXP '\\A-?[0-9]+(\\.[0-9]+)?\\z') THEN CAST(`t` AS DECIMAL(65,10)) ELSE NULL END)
===
### name: notaddressed.an-aggregate-body-declared-text-is-refused
--- note
The bare aggregate body wears the same hat. A DECLARED text field is refused by
the aggregate's own kind check -- requireNum -- so only UNKNOWN passes, exactly
as in the bool cell before the operator work closed it.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"ITEMS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "oi", "alias": "oi", "fields": {"QTY": {"table": "oi", "column": "qty", "type": "TEXT"}}, "correlate": "`oi`.`o`=`o`.`id`"}}
--- source
SUM(ITEMS, _["QTY"])
--- error
E_SQL_SHAPE
===
### name: notaddressed.an-undeclared-aggregate-body-is-not-guarded
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"ITEMS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "oi", "alias": "oi", "fields": {"QTY": {"table": "oi", "column": "qty"}}, "correlate": "`oi`.`o`=`o`.`id`"}}
--- source
SUM(ITEMS, _["QTY"])
--- expect
(SELECT COALESCE(SUM(`oi`.`qty`), 0) FROM `oi` `oi` WHERE `oi`.`o`=`o`.`id`)
===
### name: notaddressed.times-one-reaches-the-guard
--- note
The workaround §4.1a offers, and the reason it is worth writing down: `* 1` is
value-preserving in SEL, scale included, and it puts the operand through
binary(), which is where guardNumeric lives.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"ITEMS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "oi", "alias": "oi", "fields": {"QTY": {"table": "oi", "column": "qty"}}, "correlate": "`oi`.`o`=`o`.`id`"}}
--- source
SUM(ITEMS, _["QTY"] * 1)
--- expect
(SELECT COALESCE(SUM((CASE WHEN (`oi`.`qty` REGEXP '\\A-?[0-9]+(\\.[0-9]+)?\\z') THEN CAST(`oi`.`qty` AS DECIMAL(65,10)) ELSE NULL END * 1)), 0) FROM `oi` `oi` WHERE `oi`.`o`=`o`.`id`)
===
### name: warrant.raw.the-guard-evaluates-it-twice
--- note
`numericGuard` names its operand twice -- once to test it, once to cast it --
so filling it duplicates whatever the operand is. For a column that is an index
cost and nothing more: a column reference is a name, and the server reads the
same stored value both times.

A raw binding is not a name. It is arbitrary SQL the application wrote, and it
is duplicated verbatim, so the database evaluates it twice per row. Where that
expression is deterministic this is only cost. Where it is not -- RAND(), a
sequence, a UDF that touches anything -- the WHEN and the THEN can see
different values, and the guard then tests one value and casts another. Its
whole promise is "if this value is not a number, yield NULL", and that holds
only while the two are the same value.

docs/SQL-KINDS.md §6 exclusion 2 already puts raw outside the warrant. This
case is here because the exclusion excuses the CONTENT of the raw SQL and says
nothing about the layer duplicating it, and because until now nothing anywhere
recorded that it does.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"T": {"kind": "column", "raw": "nv.value", "type": "UNKNOWN"}}
--- source
T == 25
--- expect
(CASE WHEN (nv.value REGEXP '\\A-?[0-9]+(\\.[0-9]+)?\\z') THEN CAST(nv.value AS DECIMAL(65,10)) ELSE NULL END = 25)
===
### name: warrant.raw.declaring-the-type-skips-the-guard
--- note
The way out, and the same bargain a column gets: declaring the kind is what
buys the unguarded path. A raw binding declared NUM is a promise the caller
makes about their own SQL -- exclusion 1 -- and the operand is then emitted
once.

The other way out is to stop making it raw. A raw binding usually exists
because the value needed an expression rather than a column; give that
expression a name in a derived table or a CTE and bind the name, and the guard
duplicates a name instead of the expression. See §6 exclusion 2.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"T": {"kind": "column", "raw": "nv.value", "type": "NUM"}}
--- source
T == 25
--- expect
(nv.value = 25)
===
